# Pieces every site block uses (imported by Caddyfile and Caddyfile.staging).

# TLS between Cloudflare and this box. Every name is proxied by Cloudflare
# (orange cloud) with SSL/TLS "Full (strict)", and Cloudflare checks this
# certificate: a Cloudflare Origin CA certificate for myotherapp.in and
# *.myotherapp.in (SSL/TLS -> Origin Server -> Create certificate, 15 years),
# saved as /srv/moa/certs/origin.pem and origin.key (mode 600). Browsers never
# see it; they see Cloudflare's edge certificate. A name that is NOT proxied
# would need a publicly trusted certificate instead: leave `import origin_tls`
# out of its block and Caddy gets one from Let's Encrypt by itself.
#
# Optional hardening, Authenticated Origin Pulls (Cloudflare presents a client
# certificate; anything else is refused during the handshake): turn it on in
# SSL/TLS -> Origin Server, save Cloudflare's origin-pull CA as
# /srv/moa/certs/origin-pull-ca.pem, and use the block form:
#	tls {$MOA_TLS_CERT:/certs/origin.pem} {$MOA_TLS_KEY:/certs/origin.key} {
#		client_auth {
#			mode require_and_verify
#			trust_pool file /certs/origin-pull-ca.pem
#		}
#	}
(origin_tls) {
	tls {$MOA_TLS_CERT:/certs/origin.pem} {$MOA_TLS_KEY:/certs/origin.key}
}

# Response headers for every site. No `Server: Caddy`. HSTS as Vercel sent it
# (two years, this name only: no includeSubDomains, which would reach every
# club's site). Caddy writes no access log: the API logs its own requests
# with the live room's keys and tokens filtered out, and a proxy's log would
# keep them (they ride in query strings and paths).
(common) {
	header {
		# After the app's own headers are in (uvicorn names itself too).
		defer
		-Server
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=63072000"
	}
	# What nginx allowed the API (client_max_body_size 10M); the dashboards'
	# uploads go through their gateway to the same API.
	request_body {
		max_size 10MB
	}
}

# Who asked, for the app behind. {client_ip} is the visitor (global.caddy):
# X-Forwarded-For is replaced with that one address, never appended to, so a
# client cannot slip in a hop of its own. The API (app/core/net.py) and the
# site (src/server/visitor.js) both read the visitor from it. A restart (a
# deploy) holds new requests instead of failing them: a connection that
# cannot be made is retried for up to 30 s (a dial that failed sent nothing,
# so any method is safe to retry).
#
# Responses are passed on as they are written (flush_interval -1): Next
# streams a page's HTML in pieces, and an export may stream too.
(upstream) {
	header_up X-Forwarded-For {client_ip}
	header_up X-Real-IP {client_ip}
	lb_try_duration 30s
	lb_try_interval 250ms
	flush_interval -1
}
